    "synthesis": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "synthesis",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Research lower-overhead dense-DP variants: retain compact obstacle storage and a single rolling row, and measure row-major scan/update alternatives against the existing baseline. Also assess when dense DP is practical versus a sparse exact method; do not assume the sparse candidate is faster. Preserve correctness for all valid obstacle layouts, including duplicates and blocked endpoints.",
        "B": "Investigate scalable exact sparse-obstacle path counting. The measured SIMD combinatorial candidates were slower than baseline (0.0986s and 0.1046s versus 0.0807s), so do not spend effort merely increasing SIMD batch size; test serial and algorithmic alternatives, including reducing predecessor work. Account for worst-case obstacle counts and verify modular arithmetic and obstacle ordering on all valid inputs.",
        "C": "Build on the measured best candidate: the chunked OpenMP row-prefix DP ran in 0.0348s (versus 0.0807s baseline). Tune thread/chunk behavior and obstacle marking while preserving the single parallel region and two-row memory footprint. Validate blocked-cell resets, carry propagation across chunks, and small-dimension behavior; compare against serial DP across workloads rather than assuming this result generalizes.",
        "G": "Integrate only correctness-checked programs, then benchmark the actual integrated executable: candidate timings are not additive. The chunked OpenMP DP is the current measured leader; the other measured candidates were 0.0428s (sparse C), 0.0508s (compact serial A), 0.0986s and 0.1046s (SIMD B), and 0.1004s (sparse A). Compare candidates on representative sizes and obstacle patterns, retain a robust choice, and verify output against the reference across valid inputs. Do not change the target, compiler, budgets, or tests.",
        "analysis": "The task is Problem D: count monotone grid paths modulo 1,000,000,007 with blocked cells. Measurements from this run show the chunked OpenMP rolling-row DP is fastest at 0.0348s, about 2.32× faster than the 0.0807s baseline. Sparse combinatorial candidates and SIMD predecessor reductions did not improve on baseline in these measurements. Results are workload-specific: dense O(N²) DP and quadratic-in-obstacles sparse recurrences have different scaling limits, so each tree should investigate its assigned direction and G must measure the integrated result rather than infer additive gains."
